# ReCaptcha 2.0: enable Submit button on callback if recaptcha successful

Controlling the state of your form's submit button based on reCAPTCHA verification is a straightforward UI pattern that significantly improves user experience. By disabling the submit action until the invisible challenge is solved, you prevent users from spamming your backend with incomplete requests and provide immediate visual feedback that the CAPTCHA is being processed. This approach relies entirely on client-side JavaScript to listen for the `onload` callback and toggle the button's enabled state accordingly.

In a standard HTML form setup using reCAPTCHA v2, the verification logic happens asynchronously in the browser. When the user loads the page, the reCAPTCHA script initializes and triggers an `onload` event once the widget is fully rendered. At this specific moment, the invisible challenge has been presented to the user, but they have not yet solved it. This is your entry point to disable the submit button programmatically.

You can achieve this by attaching a JavaScript function to the `onload` attribute of your reCAPTCHA div or listening for the global event. Once that function executes, you simply select the submit element and set its `disabled` property to `true`. This ensures that no data is sent until the user interacts with the CAPTCHA widget.

```html
<form action="something.php" method="post">
    Name: <input type="text" size="40" name="name"><br><br>

    <!-- The onload attribute triggers immediately upon widget load -->
    <div class="g-recaptcha" data-sitekey="YOUR_SITE_KEY" 
         onmouseover="disableSubmitButton()" 
         onload="checkCaptchaStatus()">
    </div>

    <input type="submit" value="Submit" id="submit-btn">
</form>

<script>
  // This function runs when the widget is fully loaded.
  // We disable the button immediately because the challenge exists, 
  // but the user hasn't solved it yet.
  window.onload = function() {
      var btn = document.getElementById('submit-btn');
      if (btn) {
          btn.disabled = true;
      }
  };

  // This function checks if the response is ready to enable the button.
  // It is attached via a custom event listener or by checking the widget state.
  function checkCaptchaStatus() {
      var recaptchaResponse = document.getElementById('g-recaptcha-response');

      // Note: In newer implementations, you might listen for 'recaptcha-changed'
      // or simply enable on any change event if using v2 checkbox specifically.
      // For the classic v2 implementation described in source material logic:

      var btn = document.getElementById('submit-btn');
      if (btn && recaptchaResponse) {
          // If a response string exists, the user has interacted with it.
          // However, for strict "solved" states, we often listen to specific events 
          // rather than just checking existence in older DOM structures.

          // A more robust approach involves listening to the 'recaptcha-changed' event
          // which fires when the user clicks the checkbox or solves the puzzle.
      }
  }
</script>
```

A more reliable method, particularly for reCAPTCHA v2 Checkbox, is to attach an event listener to the widget container for the `recaptcha-changed` event. This event fires whenever the state of the CAPTCHA changes, such as when a user clicks the checkbox. Inside this handler, you can inspect the widget or simply enable the button if the response token has been generated.

```javascript
var recaptchaElement = document.querySelector('.g-recaptcha');
recaptchaElement.addEventListener('recaptcha-changed', function(event) {
    var btn = document.getElementById('submit-btn');
    if (btn && event.target.getResponse()) {
        btn.disabled = false;
    }
});
```

One critical trade-off to consider is the difference between "loading" and "solving." The `onload` callback indicates the widget is ready, not that it is solved. If you disable the button only after solving, you risk allowing multiple submissions while the user is thinking. Disabling it immediately upon load forces the user to engage with the CAPTCHA before attempting any submission.

Another common pitfall involves form handling in frameworks like Laravel. If your backend relies on AJAX submissions or Livewire components, the native page reload might not occur when the button is finally clicked if you aren't careful. Ensure that your `something.php` endpoint (or your controller action) validates that a valid response token was actually received and attached to the form POST data before processing the name field.

For developers looking to integrate this pattern into larger Laravel applications, resources like [https://laravelcompany.com](https://laravelcompany.com) often provide deeper dives into securing forms with enterprise-grade configurations, but the fundamental DOM manipulation logic remains consistent across vanilla JavaScript and framework environments.
